  "findings": [
    {
      "pages": [
        502,
        503,
        504,
        514
      ],
      "finding": "The February 18 surrender of posts permits troops to retain arms for departure by the coast; it is not a blanket capture of all soldiers."
    },
    {
      "pages": [
        523,
        524
      ],
      "finding": "The February 20 table divides troops by intended embarkation group. First Infantry is 443 less 20 servants and 17 laundresses = 406, not 411. All five groups total 2445. The parent aggregate counted the same return twice."
    },
    {
      "pages": [
        552
      ],
      "finding": "Waite reports two transports already sailed by April 17; seven Eighth Infantry companies and headquarters remain expected later. The February table is not physical concentration at Indianola."
    },
    {
      "pages": [
        561,
        562,
        563,
        564
      ],
      "finding": "Sibley reports two First, three Third and two Eighth Infantry companies surrendered April 25 at Saluria after unsuccessful embarkation attempts from Indianola. A detached party was captured at Indianola. No sourced per-starting-block soldier count for these subgroups is inferred."
    },
    {
      "pages": [
        567,
        568,
        569,
        572
      ],
      "finding": "Reeve reports six Eighth Infantry companies captured May 9 at San Lucas Spring after the armed opposing force was examined and resistance deemed hopeless. Van Dorn calls this the last column in Texas. Reeve reports 320 at departure and 270 bayonets on the surrender day; Van Dorn reports 10 officers and 337 men including 30 earlier prisoners. These distinct returns are not assigned to the initial nine-company bundle."
    }
  ],
  "modelScope": "A conservative whole-starting-bundle theatre exit on May 9, after an actually fired February-terms notice and only while all parts of the original contingent remain stationary in Texas. No claim every soldier departed physically, surrendered on May 9, became a prisoner on February 18, or entered a new destination unit. Capture/parole/exchange policy and prisoner treatment are not implemented.",
  "indianolaDate": "OR I-I pp.565-566, Greene May 1 report: the party left on April 24, arrived that afternoon, and surrendered early the next morning, April 25. This is distinct from Sibley?s April 25 Saluria capitulation on pp.561-564."
}
